PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation remains centered on whether the persistence of small fragments during genomic decay is primarily governed by B (the strength of genetic drift) or C (the efficiency of natural selection). While the argument for drift emphasizes stochastic residence times and the collapse of mutational target size for deletions, the disciplinary canon of genome architecture standardly evaluates the retention versus purging of excess genomic material through the drift-barrier framework. Under this framework, whether slightly deleterious or neutral non-functional fragments persist or are eliminated is fundamentally parameterized by the efficiency of natural selection ($N_e s$). Consequently, C remains the most theoretically grounded and canonically intended answer.
}

